p('Incidence-sensitive stopping bounds and a Gram obstruction for QC-GHP codes','Title2')
p('24 October 2026 | Checked stronger-bound follow-up to L-F16-stopping-bound','Small2')
p('<b>Abstract.</b> We give a positive answer to the incidence-conditioned replacement question beyond the completed B1 example. For a two-part column partition, column degree at least γ, intersection limit σ within a part and τ≥σ across parts, every nonempty stopping support satisfies 2(γ+σ)≤(σ+τ)|S|. Explicit Sidon-support circulants give sparse binary CSS families with guarantee ceiling(2(γ+1)/3); every fixed even γ admits arbitrarily long examples with a nontrivial logical class. Separately, an explicit weight-four/eight family has stopping distance and quantum logical distance exactly six at every positive multiple-of-39 lift. A repeated-constituent CSS counterpart has stopping distance two and the identical full real Gram matrix. Thus a universally valid lower bound using only that Gram data cannot certify these improvements. Every new proof and matrix/field bridge is checked in Lean.')
table([['Question / source','Answer and scope'],['Abiad-Castriota,15 July 2026, v1,Prop. 5.1,Remark 5.2,§6: useful parameters for the stated bound','Answered negatively by the previous universal theorem; unchanged here.'],['Same source: tighter spectral stopping bounds, including multi-eigenvalue refinements','A restricted obstruction: even the full real Gram matrix cannot distinguish the paired/repeated families. No universal disproof of all stronger spectral bounds.'],['This requested follow-up: incidence-conditioned replacement exceeding two on a sparse family beyond B1','YES. A reusable bound, explicit infinite families, nontrivial logical content, and a separate exact-six family.']])
p('The historical source question and this project-derived target are kept distinct. This paper adds no completed canonical author-question to the inventory. The proofs are published at an immutable source revision; all 23 Lean files, including audit programs and dependencies, have full current and pinned URLs below.','Small2')
page();h('1. The two-block stopping bound')
p('Let E(v,c) be a finite binary incidence relation. A stopping support S is nonempty and no check has exactly one selected neighbor. It need not be connected, elementary, or a stabilizer support. Partition the columns by a Boolean color. Suppose every selected column has at least γ neighbors, distinct selected columns of the same color have at most σ common neighbors, and columns of different colors have at most τ common neighbors, with σ≤τ. These hypotheses may be imposed globally to obtain a stopping-distance guarantee.')
p('<b>Theorem 1.</b> Under these assumptions,')
eq('2(γ+σ) ≤ (σ+τ)|S|.')
p('If σ+τ&gt;0 this gives the integer bound ceiling(2(γ+σ)/(σ+τ)). The division-free statement also handles zero intersection limits. Both parts are allowed to be empty individually; nonemptiness is required only for S.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> Fix a selected column v. Every check incident with v must meet another selected column. Covering its neighbors by the intersections with the other selected columns gives γ≤Σ<sub>w∈S,w≠v</sub>|N(v)∩N(w)|. Let p and q count selected columns in the two parts. A selected left column therefore gives γ+σ≤σp+τq; a selected right column gives γ+σ≤τp+σq. If both parts occur, add these inequalities. If only one occurs, the corresponding inequality gives γ+σ≤σ|S|, which is at least as strong because τ≥σ. This proves the claim. All covering, finite-sum, partition and one-part cases are checked in PartitionBound.lean.')
p('<b>Classical comparison.</b> Kashyap-Vardy [2], Theorem 1, assumes uniform column size w and maximum distinct-column intersection μ; its bound is 1+w/μ. The same count appears in the checked division-free theorem γ+μ≤μ|S|. Retaining the two parts refines this classical argument. At γ=4,σ=1,τ=2, the uniform criterion gives three and Theorem 1 gives four. The exact-six result below uses additional finite incidence information. We make no claim that the elementary covering principle is new.')
h('2. Sidon supports give verified incidence hypotheses')
p('For a finite abelian group G, call a support T Sidon when equality a+b=c+d among its elements implies equality of the unordered pairs, including repeated elements. Let A be the binary circulant with A<sub>c,j</sub>=1 exactly when c−j∈T, and set')
eq('H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A | Aᵀ].')
p('These are actual binary matrices with |G| rows and 2|G| columns. Their two column neighborhoods are j+T and j−T. A Sidon identity shows that distinct columns within one part share at most one check; across parts they share at most two. For the latter statement, after one common check c₀ is fixed, the only possible common checks are c₀ and v+w−c₀. The formal proof includes cardinality and empty-intersection cases. Every column has |T| neighbors and every row has 2|T| neighbors.')
p('Circulants commute with their transposes. Hence H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=AAᵀ+AᵀA=0 over F₂. This is precisely the scalar QC-GHP/generalized-bicycle form of [1,4], with r₁=n₁=1 and b=a. Applying Theorem 1 gives 3|S|≥2(|T|+1), without any spectral or girth assumption.')
page();h('3. An explicit sparse family for every fixed guarantee')
p('<b>Theorem 2.</b> Fix an even integer γ≥2. For any odd n≥3<super>γ</super>, use G=Z/n and')
eq('T={1,3,3²,…,3<super>γ−1</super>}, &nbsp; a(x)=Σ<sub>i=0</sub><super>γ−1</super>x<super>3ⁱ</super>,<br/>H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A | Aᵀ].')
p('Then the matrices are binary CSS checks of column weight γ and row weight 2γ. Every nonempty stopping support has size at least ceiling(2(γ+1)/3). The quantum code has a nontrivial logical class. For fixed γ, n=3<super>γ</super>(2t+1),t≥0, gives arbitrarily long matrices with the same degrees.')
p('<b>Proof of the support condition.</b> Sort the exponents in 3<super>a</super>+3<super>b</super>=3<super>c</super>+3<super>d</super>, with a≤b and c≤d. If b&lt;d, the left side is at most2·3<super>b</super>, whereas the right side is at least3<super>d</super>≥3·3<super>b</super>, a contradiction. The opposite inequality is symmetric; cancel the equal largest terms to identify the smaller exponents. Every relevant pair sum is below n, since2·3<super>γ−1</super>&lt;3<super>γ</super>≤n. Thus reduction modulo n preserves this Sidon property and all γ marks remain distinct. PowersThree.lean checks the inequalities, no-wrap cast bridge and cardinality; the support is not assumed Sidon as an unproved input.')
p('<b>Nontrivial quantum content.</b> Take the binary word equal to one on every left variable and zero on the right. Its syndrome is zero because every row meets the left block in the even number γ. The sum of its left entries is one because n is odd. Every companion row has left sum zero, and so does every linear combination of companion rows. The word is therefore outside the stabilizer span. SidonMatrices.lean checks the finite-sum, binary-field and span-separation argument.')
table([['Fixed column / row weights','New guarantee / classical uniform guarantee'],['4 / 8','4 / 3'],['6 / 12','5 / 4'],['8 / 16','6 / 5'],['General even γ / 2γ','ceiling(2(γ+1)/3) / ceiling(γ/2+1)']])
p('Choosing γ first gives any desired fixed guarantee, and then length may increase with degrees fixed. This is not a claim of growing stopping distance at fixed degree, positive asymptotic rate, or practical parameter efficiency. The displayed sufficient length is exponential in γ. The known companion-row stopping mechanism gives a size2γ upper witness; CSSStopping.lean checks that mechanism on the same binary matrices.')
h('4. A separate family with exact stopping and logical distance six')
p('<b>Theorem 3.</b> For every positive integer t, let n=39t and use a(x)=1+x+x⁴+x⁶ with H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A|Aᵀ]. There are 78t variables and 39t checks, column weight 4 and row weight 8. The stopping distance and the quantum logical distance are both exactly six.')
p('The reusable intersection criterion proves four for this entire family. The stronger exact result is certified independently: simultaneous coordinate translation reduces the base n=39 search to the two side representatives. A sound degree-one extension procedure rejects both roots with four further additions, excluding all stopping supports of size at most five. Its exact Boolean tests are reduced by Lean’s kernel. No external search result is a premise.')
page();h('4. Exact-six proof and source bridge, continued')
p('Reduction modulo39 maps lifted column lists onto their base lists, lifts every incident base check, and is injective on each check’s neighbor set. The checked local-cover theorem therefore projects a stopping support to a base stopping support of no greater size. This transfers the lower bound six to every positive39-multiple lift; it is not extrapolation from sampled lengths.')
p('A six-variable upper witness is')
eq('left(0), left(−5), left(−4),<br/>right(1), right(5), right(6).')
p('The six variables are distinct at every stated lift. Every incident check has a selected partner. A separate exact parity calculation shows that every selected check degree is even; the binary word is in the kernel. It has left weight three, whereas each companion row has left weight four. The same parity functional excludes every stabilizer combination. Every nonzero binary kernel word has stopping support, so the lower stopping bound gives logical weight at least six; the nontrivial six-word gives equality in both CSS sectors, which coincide here. Unlike the completed B1 family, this upper witness is a logical word rather than a companion row. It is also not assumed elementary: the independent base reconstruction has selected degrees zero, two and four.')
p('BicycleMatrices.lean proves the actual circulant/transpose binary matrix entries equivalent to the incidence relation and checks CSS commutation. BicycleDegrees.lean proves dimensions and degrees. The translation, kernel certificate, cover, cardinality, syndrome and stabilizer-span bridges are all included in the proof chain. The statement does not assert a formula for the logical dimension. Independent binary elimination gives k=6 for the first three sampled lifts; that is a computation recorded separately, not a new general rank theorem.')
h('5. What the full real Gram matrix cannot certify')
p('<b>Theorem 4.</b> For the same constituent circulant A, define')
eq('Good: &nbsp; H<sub>X</sub>=H<sub>Z</sub>=[A|Aᵀ];<br/>Bad: &nbsp; H′<sub>X</sub>=[A|A], &nbsp; H′<sub>Z</sub>=[Aᵀ|Aᵀ].')
p('Both pairs are binary CSS checks with the same dimensions and degrees. Over the reals,')
eq('H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>X</sub>ᵀ=AAᵀ+AᵀA=2AAᵀ=H′<sub>X</sub>H′<sub>X</sub>ᵀ.')
p('The bad X-matrix has two identical positive columns at each coordinate, yielding a stopping pair. Positive columns exclude singletons, so its stopping distance is exactly two. The good family of Theorem 3 has distance six. More generally the same identity holds for every support in Theorem 2, together with a bad stopping pair.')
p('Let L be any proposed universally valid integer lower-bound function whose inputs consist only of this real Gram matrix and the shared dimensions/degrees. Validity on the bad counterpart forces L≤2; identical input data force the same output on the good code. GramObstruction.lean, BadStopping.lean and SidonBad.lean prove this statement against the actual matrices and stopping supports. Any function of the complete Gram spectrum, or of eigenvectors determined solely by that Gram matrix, is included. An additional incidence hypothesis may distinguish the codes and is precisely what the replacement uses.')
page();h('6. Exact comparison with spectral and known stopping bounds')
p('The motivating source [1], Proposition 5.1, requires σ₂(H<sub>X</sub>)²&lt;γ/2 and separately excludes the small root branch before concluding a stopping-distance lower bound. The preceding universal parameter theorem answers that proposition’s useful-parameter question negatively in its stated scalar-factor model. That answer is preserved. Theorem 1 replaces its spectral step with incidence information; it does not claim parameters satisfying the source inequality.')
p('Zhu-Chugg [3], Corollary 6, equations 41-42, give the two regular Tanner-graph expressions below, with N variables, column degree γ, row degree k and second Gram eigenvalue η:')
eq('N(2γ−η)/(γk−η),<br/>2N(2γ+k−2−η)/(k(γk−η)).')
p('Their usual regular connected setting and strict gap η&lt;γk must be respected. If their hypotheses apply to both members of the paired/repeated comparison, validity and the identical Gram data prevent either expression from certifying more than two on the good member. If the strict gap fails, the displayed expressions are outside that regime. This comparison addresses these Gram-based guarantees; it does not dismiss every multi-eigenvector or incidence-dependent analysis in [3].')
p('For illustration only, independently reconstructed n=39 matrices have η≈27.52509838&lt;32, and the two expressions are approximately−340.3332 and−58.9375. The same reconstruction checks exact integer Gram equality and CSS commutation for t=1,…,8. Floating-point eigenvalues and sampled ranks are not premises in any theorem above.')
p('Kashyap-Vardy [2] supplies the classical uniform-overlap criterion already sufficient for a positive answer exceeding two in the weight-four case. Theorem 1 improves its value by retaining the block partition. Schwartz-Vardy [5] emphasizes that stopping distance belongs to a specified parity-check matrix and that codeword supports are stopping sets. Their stopping redundancy instead optimizes over added checks; that problem is different from our fixed-H<sub>X</sub> guarantee. The generic binary kernel/stopping bridge is checked here.')
p('Wang-Pryadko [4], Eq.8, uses H<sub>X</sub>=[A|B],H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=[B;−A]; our paired construction takes B=Aᵀ. Their distance bounds and gcd dimension formula concern logical code parameters. A logical-distance lower bound alone does not exclude non-codeword stopping supports. Kuo-Ouyang [6] studies decoders exploiting degeneracy; success of such a decoder does not establish a lower bound for ordinary peeling stopping sets. The known stabilizer upper mechanism and the new nontrivial logical upper witness are distinguished above.')
p('Public GitHub comparisons include the pinned bicycle-race README, which describes logical-distance records, and the Kuo decoder repository metadata. Bounded web searches supplied no matching full theorem for the new complete target. The X index query returned no matches; authenticated Edge inspection was unavailable because the Mac was locked. These are documented coverage limits, not proofs of absence or priority. Named inaccessible or uninspected leads remain in the source ledger.')
h('7. Evidence, inventory and remaining work')
p('The current aggregate build and all 178 named-declaration audits pass in Lean4.36.0-rc1 with mathlib5f7b4198d22a9c82e1c698a379334fe128095c82. Only propext,Classical.choice and Quot.sound occur. The project contains 19 new modules and three frozen imported research modules. Reproduce with lake build StrongerAnswer, followed by lake env lean AuditStronger.lean. The build log, complete signatures/axiom closure, source hashes and independent integer reconstruction accompany the archive.')
